    What This Course Is About
    <span class="accordion__chevron" aria-hidden="true">▼</span>
  </button>
  <div class="accordion__content">
    <div class="accordion__body">
<p>Ethics is the branch of philosophy that asks what makes actions right or wrong, what it means to live well, and what we owe to one another. These are not abstract puzzles. You face decisions with moral dimensions constantly: whether to be honest when honesty is costly, whether a law you disagree with still deserves your obedience, whether the consequences of an action matter more than the intention behind it.</p>
<p>You already have views about these questions. The purpose of this course is not to replace those views but to examine them: to put your moral intuitions in conversation with some of the most important attempts in history to systematize ethical thinking.</p>
<p>We will read six major works of ethical theory, each from the original source. The course is organized around a single running question: <em>where does morality come from, and does it have an objective foundation?</em> Each thinker gives a different answer, and the conversation between them is what makes this course worth taking.</p>
<div class="callout callout--concept"><span class="callout__icon" aria-hidden="true">💡</span><div class="callout__content">
<p>A DISTINCTION WORTH KNOWING</p>
<p>This course concentrates on <strong>ethical theory</strong> (the systematic study of what makes actions right or wrong) rather than <strong>practical ethics</strong> (debates about specific controversies like euthanasia or capital punishment). We will use practical examples to illustrate the theories, but our focus is on the thinkers and their arguments. Ethics also differs from law (not all legal requirements are moral, and not all moral obligations are legal), from etiquette (being rude is not the same as being immoral), and from religion (philosophical ethics seeks answers through reason, accessible to everyone regardless of belief).</p>
</div></div>
